{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfdgyCG2MYZm72mznCsQv","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I've written the candidate `signed_engagement_complement_symmetry`, and the self-check passed. Two gates were not checked: novelty against the live models, and convergence in the full admission fit.\n\n**What I changed:** I refined the incumbent `personal_complement_symmetry_aversion`. The only change is to its personal guessing rate. In the incumbent it can only pull a person's choices toward a coin flip. I replaced it with a signed engagement for each person, drawn from a population and ranging from −1 to 1: the choice probability is `0.5 + e·(impression − 0.5)`. Most people follow their impression of which sequence looks more random, an engagement near 0 means pure guessing, and a few people below 0 have reversed the task and pick the sequence that looks *less* random. How each sequence's randomness is judged is the incumbent's, unchanged.\n\n**Why:** The strongest critique, and the only one that survives the FDR correction (q = 0.032), is that people differ in how often they agree with the majority choice more than the incumbent produces. When I checked the data, three participants agree with the majority only 12%, 20% and 33% of the time. A guessing rate can bring agreement down to 50% at most, so it cannot explain those three.\n\n**Self-check:** it printed `OK`, with an ELPD-LOO of −3467.8 on the 100-draw, one-chain smoke fit. The incumbent's full fit is at −3465.7, but the two numbers come from different fits and can't be compared directly. Two warnings came up:\n- **PSIS-LOO reliability:** 9.2% of trials had Pareto k above 0.5, against a 1% tolerance. A fit this short may cause much of that, but if it persists in the full fit, admission would treat the ELPD-LOO estimate as untrustworthy.\n- **R-hat:** it could not be reported from a single chain, so convergence is still unverified.\n\n**Novelty:** 